{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790962350,"nonce":"nU4aa0XsdE55jcr3KAi3t6753P4=","title":"Signals and computeds are never released from Blinc's graph","message":"A signal or computed handle is freed by its GC finalizer, but its entry in\nBlinc's reactive graph, and every property binding on it, stays forever:\nBlinc cannot dispose them (blinc 5e0f09f56ab95dc2a0fe9f89946473b41f8a9cc7724f64a544b3e9df035220bc).\nA computed's Haxe closure is rooted by that graph entry, so it and everything\nit captures stays alive too.\n\nMeasured with 50k Signal.make per round and hl.Gc.major between rounds, on\nstock HashLink 1.15 (nuc, Linux x86_64): RSS 12, 18, 25, 31, 37, 44, 50,\n56 MB. The same loop with LayoutTree or Color handles is flat.\n\nPlan once Blinc can dispose: the finalizer queues the id rather than\ndisposing (stock hl runs finalizers inside the collection, where Blinc's\ngraph lock may already be held by a computation), and Tree::flush, or the\nnext native entry, drains the queue through Blinc's dispose. Dropping the\nderived drops its Rooted closure outside the collector, which is where\nhl_remove_root is allowed.","files":null}]}